{
  "version": 1,
  "about": [
    "The golden fixture for the device seal (mls/core/src/device_seal.rs; not part of this specification): one 226-byte box per recipient device, beside a version 3 envelope, that names the group, the sender's device key and the epoch envelope secret, so a device whose copy forked or fell behind keeps the envelope and opens it once it is back.",
    "seal = 01 || E || c1 || c2. E = X25519(e, 9); k1 = HKDF-SHA256(X25519(e, R), \"peachy device seal v1 sender\" || 00 || E || R_ed); c1 = AES-256-GCM(k1, zero nonce, S_ed, aad 01 || E); k2 = HKDF-SHA256(X25519(e, R) || X25519(s, R), \"peachy device seal v1 body\" || 00 || E || R_ed || S_ed || c1); c2 = AES-256-GCM(k2, zero nonce, body, aad 01 || E || c1); body = len || group id zero-padded to 64 || epoch secret (32) || SHA-256(envelope) (32). Ed25519 device keys serve as X25519: the public key through the birational map, the secret the first half of SHA-512 over the seed. HKDF has an empty salt.",
    "A key, the recipient's or the sender's, is taken only in its one canonical spelling, on the curve, and of the prime order: no small-order or mixed-order key, and no y at or past p.",
    "`open` answers every refusal as `opened: false`, and `seal` as `refused: true`, so every replay compares one object; the product door gives one opaque error for all of them.",
    "Every build reads seals, and `constants.sends` is false: no build writes one.",
    "Written by an independent implementation (Python's hashlib, the cryptography package's Ed25519, X25519, HKDF and AES-GCM, and the Edwards arithmetic by hand), so the Rust replay is a second opinion. Replayed through the fixture door (kind \"device_seal\") in Rust, JavaScript and Swift (and, in Peachy's Android client, Kotlin)."
  ],
